  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 PM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\n\nCurrently a very deep trough in Nevada is causing southwest flow\nover the Northern Plains. East of the Mississippi River is\na ridge pattern. At the surface a low pressure system is \nworking into the western Dakotas, with widespread rain to its \neast along a stationary front. This front is located just south\nof Interstate 94 from west to east. Along the frontal zone, \nseveral inches of rain has fallen between the Missouri River and\nAshley, ND. For this reason a Flood Advisory has been issued \nuntil 3pm. Western North Dakota is now in the dry slot of the \nlow. \n\nThis evening model guidance suggests a redevelopment of showers\nand maybe a thunderstorm in the south central and east. Models \nhave very little MUCAPE, less than 400 J/kg, this evening and \novernight. The rain shower development will likely be along that\nstationary front as it slowly slides north in the surface \ncyclonic flow. On the backside of the low, fog may be possible\nacross the forecast area until the late morning.  \n\nThe rest of this week the pattern will change to zonal flow \nover the Northern Plains, allowing all this rain that just fell \nto dry out. No rain is then in the forecast until this weekend \nwhen an Alberta Clipper slides into the state Friday night. \nTemperatures through the week will stay near normal in the 60s \nto lower 70s as the zonal flow keeps things steady.",
